From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: setupapi: reading a driver key does not need the right to create under HKLM

open_driver_key opened HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Class with
RegCreateKeyEx(KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY) even when only reading a driver key. A
standard user may not create under HKLM, so on the shared machine prefix every
SetupDiOpenDevRegKey(DIREG_DRV, KEY_READ) failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED --
found running Audacity 4 as a standard user ("Failed to open driver class
root key, error 5"). Windows lets a standard user read driver keys. The class
root is now only opened; create_driver_key still creates it when needed.

Gate: make test-drvkey (the Class key locked read-only, a present device's
driver key must still open for reading).
--- a/dlls/setupapi/devinst.c
+++ b/dlls/setupapi/devinst.c
@@ -605,8 +605,11 @@
     DWORD size = sizeof(path);
     LONG l;
 
-    if ((l = RegCreateKeyExW(HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, ControlClass, 0, NULL, 0,
-            KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY, NULL, &class_key, NULL)))
+    /* Stained Glass OS: opening a driver key only opens the class root, it
+     * does not create under it -- a standard user may read driver keys, as on
+     * Windows, and asking for KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY on HKLM refused every such
+     * read with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (Audacity's audio device enumeration). */
+    if ((l = RegOpenKeyExW(HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, ControlClass, 0, KEY_ENUMERATE_SUB_KEYS, &class_key)))
     {
         ERR("Failed to open driver class root key, error %lu.\n", l);
         return l;
